  title: 'Capacity Determination — Patient Refusing Life-Saving Treatment',
  url: '',
  sections: [
    Section(
      title: 'Initial Presentation',
      content:
          'A 65-year-old male was admitted to acute inpatient rehabilitation 10 days ago following a C5 ASIA C incomplete spinal cord injury from a ground-level fall off a ladder while cleaning gutters. He underwent emergent anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (C4-C6). Post-operatively, he has 2/5 to 3/5 strength in upper extremities and 1/5 to 2/5 in lower extremities with intact but reduced sensation below C5 (ASIA C). He was making slow but measurable functional gains in the first week — improved sitting balance, minimal gains in upper extremity function. Today, he refuses to get out of bed for therapy, refuses his medications, and tells the nurse he wants to leave the hospital against medical advice. When the attending physiatrist arrives, he states: "I have thought about this carefully. I would rather die than live like this. Let me go home. I am done."',
