    Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content:
          '**Past Medical History:** Hypertension, type 2 diabetes, benign prostatic hyperplasia, mild cognitive impairment diagnosed 2 years ago (MMSE 25/30 at that time), remote history of depression treated with sertraline 15 years ago (discontinued after 2 years). No prior suicidal ideation or attempts.\n\n**Pre-Injury Function:** Independent in all ADLs and IADLs. Retired engineer. Active — golfed 3x/week, maintained his own home and yard. Drove independently. Volunteered at a local food bank. Described by family as "fiercely independent" and "hates asking for help."\n\n**Current Functional Status:** Moderate to maximal assist for all transfers, dependent for lower body ADLs, requires setup assist for feeding (tenodesis grasp developing but weak), neurogenic bladder (indwelling catheter, planned transition to intermittent catheterization), neurogenic bowel (digital stimulation program initiated).\n\n**Current Medications:** Lisinopril 20 mg daily, metformin 500 mg BID, gabapentin 300 mg TID, docusate/senna, famotidine, subcutaneous heparin, melatonin (refusing all as of this morning).\n\n**Social History:** Widowed 3 years ago (wife died of ovarian cancer). Two adult daughters — one lives locally (primary support), one lives out of state. Lives alone in a two-story home he built himself. Active member of Rotary Club. Strong social network but has been declining visitors since admission. His wife\'s death was described by his daughter as "devastating — he was never the same after."\n\n**Behavioral Changes Noted by Staff:** Initially engaged in therapy (days 1-5). Became withdrawn after a difficult bowel care session on day 6 where he was "humiliated." Has been declining therapy sessions since day 7. Sleeping poorly. Eating less than 50% of meals. Told his daughter yesterday, "Your mother was the lucky one."',
