    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING',
      content:
          '**Capacity Evaluation — Appelbaum Four-Component Model:**\n\nDecisional capacity is decision-specific and must be assessed systematically:\n\n**1. Understanding:** Can the patient comprehend the relevant information?\n- Test: "Can you tell me what the doctors have told you about your injury and your potential for recovery?"\n- He must demonstrate understanding of: his SCI diagnosis, the ASIA C incomplete status with potential for further neurological recovery, the goals and expected benefits of rehabilitation, the consequences of leaving AMA (medical complications, loss of insurance coverage, skin breakdown, UTI, DVT risk).\n- If he states "I am paralyzed and will never walk" — he may have an inaccurate understanding that affects his decision.\n\n**2. Appreciation:** Can the patient apply that information to his own situation?\n- Test: "Do you believe the information about recovery potential applies to you?"\n- He must appreciate that incomplete SCI (ASIA C) has significantly better prognosis than complete injury, and that continued rehabilitation could meaningfully improve his function.\n- If he says "Maybe others get better, but I won\'t" — this may reflect depressive cognitive distortion rather than rational assessment.\n\n**3. Reasoning:** Can the patient weigh alternatives rationally?\n- Test: "Can you explain how you reached your decision?"\n- He should demonstrate consideration of pros and cons, alternative options, and the impact on people he cares about.\n- Depressive reasoning may present as inability to envision any positive future — "All-or-nothing thinking" (if I cannot be fully independent, life is not worth living).\n\n**4. Expression of Choice:** Can the patient clearly and consistently communicate a decision?\n- He has clearly expressed a choice. This criterion is met.\n\n**Key Question: Does he HAVE capacity, or is depression IMPAIRING capacity?**\n- PHQ-9 of 22 with active suicidal ideation represents severe major depression\n- Severe depression can impair appreciation and reasoning without affecting understanding or choice expression\n- Pre-existing mild cognitive impairment may further complicate assessment\n- His capacity is questionable — formal psychiatric consultation is warranted BEFORE accepting his refusal as a capacitated decision\n\n**TBI Screening:**\n- Ground-level fall with head/neck trauma — was there loss of consciousness?\n- Review imaging for intracranial pathology\n- Mild TBI can impair judgment, increase impulsivity, and worsen depression\n- If no head imaging obtained, consider CT head',
