    Section(
      title: '+ Diagnostic question',
      content:
          '**Q: The patient tells you, "I understand my injury is incomplete. I know I might get some movement back. But I don\'t care. Living like this is worse than dying." Does this statement demonstrate capacity?**\n\n**A:** This statement satisfies understanding but raises significant concerns about appreciation and reasoning:\n\n**Understanding: Adequate.** He correctly identifies his injury as incomplete with recovery potential.\n\n**Appreciation: Questionable.** His statement "I don\'t care" about recovery potential suggests he may not be able to appreciate the personal relevance of the medical information. A person with intact appreciation would be able to weigh the possibility of meaningful recovery against their current suffering. His inability to assign value to future recovery may reflect **depressive anhedonia and hopelessness** rather than a rational values-based decision.\n\n**Reasoning: Impaired.** The hallmark of depressive reasoning is **cognitive constriction** — the inability to envision any future other than the current state of suffering. A capacitated patient refusing treatment would typically say something like "I have considered the possibility of recovery, and even if I regain some function, the life I would have is not one I want for these specific reasons." His blanket "I don\'t care" suggests foreclosure rather than reasoned deliberation.\n\n**Clinical judgment:** This patient likely has **depression-impaired decisional capacity** for this specific decision (leaving AMA). He may have capacity for other decisions (choosing meals, declining a specific therapy session). Capacity is decision-specific and severity-proportional — the higher the stakes, the higher the bar for capacity.\n\n**Next step:** He cannot be held involuntarily solely for refusing rehabilitation. However, active suicidal ideation (PHQ-9 item 9, "your mother was the lucky one") triggers a duty to assess for imminent danger and initiate psychiatric evaluation. This is not about overriding autonomy — it is about ensuring his refusal reflects his authentic values rather than a treatable psychiatric illness.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ Challenge question',
      content:
          '**Q: The psychiatrist evaluates him and concludes he has capacity to refuse rehabilitation. He wants to leave AMA. What are your obligations?**\n\n**A:** If a psychiatrist confirms capacity after thorough evaluation (including depression treatment discussion), the following obligations apply:\n\n1. **Respect his autonomy:** A capacitated patient has the right to refuse any treatment, including rehabilitation, even if the medical team disagrees. This is a fundamental ethical and legal principle.\n\n2. **However, capacity does not end the conversation:**\n- **Treat the depression first:** Request that the patient agree to a trial of antidepressant treatment (e.g., 2-4 weeks of SSRI/SNRI) before making an irreversible decision. Frame this as: "I respect your right to leave. But depression is a treatable medical condition that can change how you see the world. Would you be willing to try treatment for the depression before making this decision? If after treatment you still feel the same way, I will support your choice."\n- **Motivational interviewing:** Explore ambivalence. Use open-ended questions: "What would your life need to look like for you to want to continue?" This may reveal modifiable barriers (humiliation from bowel care, loss of independence, unresolved grief).\n- **Peer support:** Offer connection with SCI peer mentor — someone with similar injury level who has been through the same despair and recovered. Peer support is the single most effective intervention for SCI adjustment.\n\n3. **If he insists on leaving AMA:**\n- Complete AMA documentation including risks explained (DVT, pneumonia, pressure injury, UTI, loss of recovery potential, death)\n- Ensure safe discharge plan (even AMA patients deserve a safety plan): home health referral, primary care follow-up, psychiatric follow-up, DVT prophylaxis prescription, emergency contact plan\n- Prescribe antidepressant even if he refuses inpatient treatment\n- Provide crisis hotline numbers (988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline)\n- Contact his daughters with his permission (or without if imminent danger)\n- Document thoroughly: capacity assessment, risks discussed, patient\'s responses, discharge plan, follow-up arranged',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT',
      content:
          '**Psychiatric Consultation (Urgent — Same Day):**\n- Formal capacity evaluation by psychiatry, specifically addressing depression\'s impact on decision-making\n- Suicide risk assessment (Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale)\n- Evaluate for adjustment disorder vs major depressive episode vs complicated grief (wife\'s death)\n- Medication recommendations: consider mirtazapine (benefits appetite, sleep, and mood — all impaired in this patient) or sertraline (prior positive response)\n- Assess whether involuntary hold criteria are met (state-specific — generally requires imminent danger to self or others)\n\n**Motivational Enhancement Strategies:**\n- **Identify the trigger:** The humiliating bowel care session on day 6 was the inflection point. Address this directly — "I heard the bowel care was a very difficult experience. Can you tell me about it?" Modify the bowel program to maximize dignity (same-gender aide, curtain privacy, scheduled timing, patient control of process).\n- **Restore agency:** He is a retired engineer who built his own house. Incorporate problem-solving and control into his rehab — let him direct his therapy schedule, choose therapy goals, make decisions about his care plan.\n- **Small wins:** Set achievable daily goals that demonstrate progress — self-feeding, electric wheelchair trialing, using phone independently. Counteract hopelessness with tangible evidence of function.\n\n**Addressing Modifiable Factors:**\n- **Pain:** Gabapentin may be inadequate — reassess pain level and optimize (neuropathic pain can drive depression)\n- **Sleep:** Insomnia worsens depression — optimize sleep hygiene, consider trazodone 50 mg qHS\n- **Grief:** Unresolved grief for wife — the SCI may have reactivated grief by making him feel "closer to death." Grief counseling referral.\n- **Isolation:** He is declining visitors — gently encourage his daughter to visit, facilitate phone calls with Rotary friends. Social isolation deepens depression.\n- **Catheter:** Indwelling catheter is a visible reminder of dependency — accelerate transition to intermittent catheterization or discuss options for autonomy.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ Management question',
      content:
          '**Q: He agrees to stay but refuses antidepressant medication, saying "I\'m not depressed, I\'m realistic." He will continue rehab but remains passively engaged. How do you proceed?**\n\n**A:** This is a common and challenging scenario — the patient conflates depressive hopelessness with realism:\n\n1. **Respect his medication refusal for now** but continue psychiatric follow-up. Forced medication requires a higher legal threshold than is present here.\n\n2. **Reframe the discussion:** Avoid arguing about whether he is "depressed." Instead: "You may be right that what you are feeling is a realistic response to a terrible situation. But here is what I observe — you are not sleeping, not eating, not enjoying visits from your daughters, and you cannot imagine any future that is worth living. Whether we call that depression or a realistic reaction, those symptoms are treatable and treating them will help you think more clearly about your options."\n\n3. **Non-pharmacological depression interventions:**\n- **Behavioral activation:** Structured daily schedule with pleasant activities (not just therapy). TV sports, audiobooks, phone calls with friends.\n- **Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT):** Psychology consult for targeted intervention on hopelessness and all-or-nothing thinking.\n- **Peer mentoring:** The single most powerful intervention. A person with similar SCI level who has rebuilt a meaningful life can challenge his belief system in ways the medical team cannot.\n- **Chaplaincy/spiritual care:** If he has any spiritual framework, this resource can be valuable for existential distress.\n\n4. **Monitor closely:** Daily safety assessment (nursing to report any suicidal statements). Reassess PHQ-9 weekly. If depression worsens or suicidal ideation becomes more active (plan, intent, means), re-initiate urgent psychiatric evaluation and consider involuntary hold.\n\n5. **Set a revisiting point:** "I would like to make an agreement with you. Stay with us for two more weeks, participate in therapy to the best of your ability, and let us address your sleep and pain. In two weeks, we will have an honest conversation about how you feel and what you want to do next. Deal?"',
