    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE',
      content:
          '**Ethics Committee Consultation:**\n- Appropriate when there is conflict between patient autonomy and beneficence, and the clinical team is uncertain how to proceed\n- Ethics committee role: provide a structured framework for the clinical and ethical dilemma, review capacity assessment adequacy, ensure patient rights are protected while exploring all options to preserve life\n- Ethics consultation does NOT override the patient\'s autonomous decision — it provides guidance and documentation\n- Document the ethics consultation findings in the medical record\n\n**Legal Standards for Capacity:**\n- **Capacity vs competence:** Capacity is a clinical determination made by physicians. Competence is a legal determination made by courts. Physicians assess capacity; only judges declare incompetence.\n- **State-specific involuntary hold criteria:** Generally requires imminent danger to self or others due to mental illness. Passive suicidal ideation alone may not meet threshold. Active suicidal ideation with plan and intent typically does.\n- **Guardianship:** If the patient lacks capacity and there is no advance directive, temporary guardianship could be sought through the court. His daughter may petition. However, this is a slow process and should be a last resort.\n- **Advance directives:** Does he have a living will or healthcare power of attorney? If so, review them. If not, this is an important conversation once depression is addressed.\n\n**AMA Discharge Protocol (If Ultimately Pursued):**\n1. Documented capacity evaluation by psychiatrist\n2. Informed refusal: risks of AMA discharge explained and documented (specific to SCI: autonomic dysreflexia, DVT/PE, pressure injury, UTI/sepsis, respiratory compromise)\n3. Patient signs AMA form (if willing) — refusal to sign does not prevent discharge\n4. Safe discharge plan regardless of AMA status: prescriptions, home health, DME, follow-up appointments, emergency numbers\n5. Continue insurance coverage — contrary to common belief, AMA discharge does NOT automatically void insurance. However, some insurers may challenge coverage.\n6. Risk management notification\n7. Follow-up phone call within 24-48 hours\n\n**Documentation Requirements:**\n- Detailed capacity assessment with Appelbaum criteria addressed\n- Psychiatric consultation note\n- Ethics committee consultation note (if obtained)\n- All conversations with patient regarding risks, alternatives, and decision\n- Family communication and involvement\n- Safety assessment and plan\n- Everything the team did to address modifiable factors',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ Systems question',
      content:
          '**Q: His local daughter wants to pursue guardianship to keep him in rehab. His out-of-state daughter disagrees and says "Dad has always been clear he would never want to live dependent on others." How do you navigate this family disagreement?**\n\n**A:** This family conflict adds a layer of complexity that requires careful management:\n\n1. **Do not take sides between family members.** Both perspectives are valid — one is prioritizing preservation of life, the other is honoring the patient\'s expressed values.\n\n2. **Clarify the medical/legal reality:**\n- Guardianship requires a court determination of legal incompetence, not just family disagreement\n- A psychiatrist has assessed him as having capacity — this makes guardianship difficult to obtain legally\n- Even if granted, a guardian is expected to make decisions the patient WOULD make, not what the guardian wants — this is the substituted judgment standard\n- If the patient has capacity, guardianship is inappropriate and could constitute unlawful restraint\n\n3. **Facilitate a family meeting (with the patient present and consenting):**\n- Include both daughters (in-person and video), the patient, psychiatry, social work, and the treatment team\n- Allow each person to express their perspective without interruption\n- Present the medical facts: ASIA C with recovery potential, treatable depression, modifiable barriers\n- Clarify the ethical framework: respect for autonomy does not mean abandoning the patient — it means ensuring the decision is truly his and not the depression\'s\n\n4. **Explore advance directives:** Ask the patient directly, in front of both daughters: "Before this injury, did you ever discuss with your family what you would want in a situation like this? Did you ever write anything down?" This grounds the conversation in the patient\'s own prior expressed values.\n\n5. **Recommend against guardianship at this time:** Document that the patient has been assessed as having capacity, that depression treatment has been offered, and that the clinical team is working to address modifiable factors. Guardianship should only be pursued if capacity is clearly absent, and that is not the current clinical determination.\n\n6. **Support both daughters:** Refer both to social work for caregiver support. The local daughter needs respite and emotional support. The out-of-state daughter needs to feel heard despite her physical distance.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          '**Scenario:** You enter the patient\'s room. He is lying in bed staring at the ceiling. He says: "Doc, I have thought about this. I would rather die than live like this. I built my house with my own hands. I coached Little League. I took care of my wife until she died. Now I can\'t even wipe my own rear end. This is not living. Please just let me go."\n\n**This is the Highest-Stakes Communication in Rehabilitation Medicine:**\n\n**Step 1 — Stop. Sit down. Be fully present.** Do not stand over him. Pull a chair to his bedside. Make eye contact. Give him silence after he finishes speaking. This moment deserves your undivided attention.\n\n**Step 2 — Validate without agreeing or dismissing:**\n"I hear you. What you are describing — the loss of the things that made you who you are — is one of the most painful experiences a person can go through. I am not going to pretend I fully understand what this feels like, because I don\'t. But I believe you when you say it is unbearable right now."\n\n**Step 3 — Gently explore the suicidal statement:**\n"When you say you would rather die than live like this, I need to understand what you mean. Are you saying that life feels so painful right now that death seems preferable? Or are you telling me you are thinking about ending your life? Those are different things, and both matter."\n\n*If he endorses active suicidal ideation with plan or intent, this becomes an acute psychiatric emergency — activate 1:1 observation and psychiatric consultation immediately.*\n\n*If he endorses passive ideation (wishes he were dead but no plan to act), continue the conversation:*\n\n**Step 4 — Introduce doubt without invalidating:**\n"I am not going to argue with how you feel right now. But I want to share something I have seen in my career: almost every patient with a new spinal cord injury who tells me they would rather die is telling me about how they feel in this moment — not about how they will feel in six months or a year. And the research confirms this — the vast majority of SCI patients who report wanting to die in the acute phase later report being glad they survived.\n\nYou are making the biggest decision of your life during the worst week of your life, while you are grieving your wife, not sleeping, in pain, and dealing with experiences that would shake anyone. I am not asking you to be optimistic. I am asking you to consider the possibility that how you feel right now might not be permanent."\n\n**Step 5 — Connect to his identity:**\n"You told me you built your house with your own hands. That tells me you are someone who solves problems, who does not give up easily. I am asking you to apply that same toughness to this situation — not forever, just for a few more weeks. Give us time to treat the things that are making this unbearable — the pain, the sleeplessness, the bowel care that was humiliating. Let us fix what we can fix."\n\n**Step 6 — Make a concrete ask:**\n"Here is what I am asking: two more weeks. Not forever. Two weeks where we address the pain, the sleep, the dignity issues with bowel care. I will bring in someone who has been exactly where you are — a man with a similar injury who felt the same way and can tell you honestly what life looked like a year later. At the end of two weeks, we talk again. If you still feel the same way after we have addressed everything we can address, I will respect that. Deal?"',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ Communication question',
      content:
          '**Q: His daughter is in the hallway crying. She grabs your arm and says, "He told me he wished he had died in the fall. You have to do something. Can\'t you keep him here? He\'s not thinking straight." How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** This requires balancing family distress, patient rights, and safety obligations:\n\n"I can see how frightened you are, and I want you to know that I am taking this very seriously. Your father\'s statement about wishing he had died concerns me too, and I have already arranged for a psychiatrist to evaluate him today.\n\nLet me be honest with you about where things stand:\n\nFirst, your father is not in immediate danger right now. He is in a hospital with 24-hour nursing care and medical staff who are aware of his statements. I have placed him on closer monitoring as a precaution.\n\nSecond, I believe your father is severely depressed — his symptoms clearly meet criteria for major depression. Depression is a medical illness that can change how a person thinks and what they believe about the future. It is treatable. The challenge is that he does not see himself as depressed — he sees himself as realistic.\n\nThird, I have to be transparent about the limits of what I can do. If a psychiatrist determines your father has the mental capacity to make his own decisions, I cannot legally force him to stay in the hospital against his will. I know that is not what you want to hear, and it is not what I want either.\n\nBut here is what I CAN do:\n- I can treat his depression aggressively, even if he resists medication — there are therapy approaches that work.\n- I can address the specific things making him miserable: the bowel care humiliation, the pain, the sleeplessness.\n- I can connect him with someone who has lived through exactly what he is experiencing.\n- I can buy time by engaging him in small, meaningful activities and goals.\n- I can keep having honest conversations with him.\n\nI need your help too. Your presence matters more than you know. Even if he does not show it, you being here reminds him that his life has meaning to people he loves. Keep coming. Keep talking to him about normal things — not just about his injury.\n\nAnd I want to check on you as well. Watching a parent go through this is traumatic. Do you have anyone supporting you — a friend, a counselor, your sister? Our social worker can connect you with family support resources.\n\nI promise you I will not give up on your father. But I need you to trust the process and give us a little time."',
